# WELCOME P0 — environment variables, for a self-hosted instance.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local     # .env.local is gitignored; never commit it
#
# The app boots with ONLY the five REQUIRED variables. Every other variable turns
# ONE optional feature on, and its absence is reported honestly rather than
# simulated: email falls back to a local log file, Telegram is simply not
# configured, the AI enrichment provider answers `disabled`, the Google/Microsoft
# rows in `GET /api/providers` answer `not_configured`. Nothing here belongs to
# the upstream author — every value is one you create yourself. See
# SELF_HOSTING.md for the full walkthrough, and
# tests/unit/env-example-coverage.test.ts for the gate that keeps this file in
# sync with the code.
#
# Labels used below:
#   REQUIRED            — no value, no working app.
#   OPTIONAL (<thing>)  — enables <thing>; unset = <thing> is visibly off.
#
# Values in this file are placeholders that are safe to publish. Do NOT paste a
# real key here (the file is tracked): a credential-shaped value fails
# `pnpm scan:secrets`, which runs in CI and in `pnpm gates`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED. development | production | test.
# `production` switches on fail-closed behaviour: no dev OTP exposure, no mock
# transports, no dev-log fallbacks; missing providers answer 503 instead of
# pretending. Use `development` for a local instance — `pnpm db:reset` and
# `pnpm db:seed` refuse to run under `production`.
APP_ENV=development

# REQUIRED. Absolute public origin of YOUR deployment. Used to build public card
# links, QR payloads, share metadata and the Telegram webhook URL.
# Local: http://localhost:3000 · Deployed: https://your-domain.example
APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# REQUIRED. PostgreSQL connection string. PostgreSQL 16+ (the schema is plain
# SQL; no extensions are required).
#   createdb welcome_dev   →   postgres://localhost:5432/welcome_dev
# Managed Postgres (Neon, Supabase, RDS…) works too: append ?sslmode=require.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/welcome_dev

# REQUIRED. Base64 of EXACTLY 32 random bytes: the AES-256-GCM key that encrypts
# contact fields at rest. Generate your own; never reuse one across deployments
# and never lose it (existing rows cannot be decrypted without it).
#   openssl rand -base64 32
ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# REQUIRED. Pepper (>= 8 chars, make it long and random) for the HMAC-SHA256
# email-lookup hash and for OTP hashing. Generate your own:
#   openssl rand -base64 24
HASH_PEPPER=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL (database)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OPTIONAL (database). How many MILLISECONDS one SQL statement may run before
# PostgreSQL cancels it. Default: 10000 — 10 seconds, the same budget this app
# gives a single outbound HTTP call (src/integrations/*/transport.ts), so a hung
# statement cannot outlive the request or worker tick waiting on it.
# It is a SERVER-side guard, not a client-side deadline: PostgreSQL enforces it,
# so it also covers a statement parked on a lock — where the client is healthy
# and would otherwise wait forever — and it releases the pooled connection
# (10 of them, `max` in src/lib/db.ts) instead of leaking it.
# Raise it only if YOUR workload has a legitimately slow statement. The accepted
# range is [100, 600000] (0.1s … 10min); anything else — including 0, which
# PostgreSQL reads as "no limit" — is IGNORED in favour of the default, so this
# cannot be switched off by a typo, a blank value or a seconds/milliseconds
# mix-up. Migrations are outside this budget (scripts/migrate.mjs builds its own
# client), so a long index build is unaffected.
STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL (email sign-in and notifications)
#
# Without these, one-time login codes are written to the gitignored
# .runtime/otp.log (development) — enough to walk the whole product locally.
# In production with no key the OTP route answers an honest
# 503 `email_channel_disabled`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OPTIONAL (email). API key from resend.com → API Keys (the free tier is enough
# for a personal instance). Setting it switches OTP delivery to real email.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# OPTIONAL (email). From-address for outbound mail. Resend only accepts a domain
# you verified in their dashboard; leave it unset and the code falls back to
# Resend's sandbox sender, which can mail only the account owner — fine for a
# smoke test, not for real users. Format: `WELCOME <no-reply@your-domain.example>`
RESEND_FROM=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL (Telegram channel)
#
# Unset = the Telegram channel is disabled: outbox jobs are suppressed with an
# auditable `channel_disabled` outcome, and production NEVER falls back to a mock
# (enforced by tests).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OPTIONAL (Telegram). Bot token from @BotFather → /newbot.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# OPTIONAL (Telegram). The @username BotFather assigned — used to build deep
# links. Falls back to a placeholder name when unset.
TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=

# OPTIONAL (Telegram). Random string you invent; the webhook route checks it
# constant-time against the `x-telegram-bot-api-secret-token` header. Pass the
# same value to setWebhook:
#   curl -X POST "https://api.telegram.org/bot$TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN/setWebhook" \
#     -d "url=$APP_BASE_URL/api/webhooks/telegram" \
#     -d "secret_token=$TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET"
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# OPTIONAL (Telegram, development/test only). `1` selects the in-process mock
# transport so a local worker can be exercised without a bot. Ignored in
# production (a mock is never selectable there).
TELEGRAM_MOCK=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL (AI profile enrichment — Google Vertex AI / Gemini)
#
# Needs BOTH a provider switch and credentials. Unset, or `vertex` without
# credentials, = the provider reports `disabled` and POST /api/me/enrich answers
# 503 `enrichment_disabled`/`enrichment_not_configured` instead of pretending.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OPTIONAL (enrichment). `vertex` (or `vertex-gemini`) to enable;
# `mock` in development/test for offline runs; anything else = disabled.
ENRICHMENT_PROVIDER=

# OPTIONAL (enrichment). Your own GCP project id with the Vertex AI API enabled.
GCP_PROJECT_ID=

# OPTIONAL (enrichment). Credential source 1: a short-lived access token.
#   gcloud auth application-default print-access-token
# The code only reads the variable; it never shells out to gcloud.
GCP_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# OPTIONAL (enrichment). Credential source 2: a service-account key, as raw JSON
# or base64 of that JSON (auto-detected). The key signs a local JWT — no token
# endpoint is called. Treat this value as you would a password.
GCP_SA_JSON=

# OPTIONAL (enrichment). Alias for GCP_SA_JSON (the deploy secret files use it).
GCP_SA_JSON_B64=

# OPTIONAL (enrichment). Vertex location. Default: global.
GCP_LOCATION=

# OPTIONAL (enrichment). Model id. Default: gemini-2.5-flash (the id verified
# against Vertex AI; other ids 404 in some projects).
GCP_MODEL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL (Google Contacts / Google Calendar)
#
# Your own OAuth client. Unset = both Google rows report `not_configured` in
# GET /api/providers and the connect controls on /me/connections stay unavailable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OPTIONAL (Google). Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials →
# OAuth client ID → Web application. Authorised redirect URI (exact):
#   $APP_BASE_URL/api/oauth/google/callback
# Enable the People API and the Google Calendar API on the project.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=

# OPTIONAL (Google). The client secret that belongs with the client id above.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL (post-event follow-up)
#
# Two message-sending mechanics, each with its own switch, both OFF by default:
# absent means "the feature does not exist" — no scan, no job, no toggle, no
# endpoint. They are read on every call, so flipping one takes effect on the very
# next worker tick.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OPTIONAL (follow-up). `true` enables the «next step» reminder scan.
FOLLOWUP_REMINDERS_ENABLED=

# OPTIONAL (follow-up). `true` enables the weekly «who to meet» digest.
DIGEST_ENABLED=

# OPTIONAL (follow-up). Days to wait before a next_step reminder is due.
# Default: 7. A non-integer, zero or negative value falls back to the default.
FOLLOWUP_REMINDER_DAYS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL (operational identity and limits)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OPTIONAL (operator identity). The contact address your deployment publishes:
# the landing page's pilot CTA and the legal pages. UNSET = no address is
# published and the pilot CTAs are not rendered at all. Set it to YOUR inbox —
# it is never inherited from the upstream author.
OPERATOR_CONTACT_EMAIL=

# OPTIONAL (worker). Shared secret for POST|GET /api/internal/worker-tick, the
# one-tick endpoint a serverless deployment (or cron) uses instead of the
# long-running `pnpm worker`. Compared constant-time; unset in production → 401
# (fail-closed). On Vercel also set CRON_SECRET to the SAME value, because the
# cron invoker sends it as `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`.
WORKER_TICK_SECRET=

# OPTIONAL (health). How many SECONDS a worker heartbeat may age before
# `GET /api/health` reports `"worker":"down"`.
# Default: 93600 — 26 hours, i.e. one daily cron period (the free Vercel plan
# allows nothing faster, and vercel.json ships a daily cron) plus 2 hours of
# slack for cron dispatch jitter. The default is deliberately shaped to a tick
# CADENCE: a window shorter than one tick period reports a healthy worker as
# down, every day, forever — which is how an operator learns to ignore the
# field. It stays below two periods, so a worker that misses a whole day is
# still reported down.
# Set it to YOUR cadence plus slack if yours differs: `pnpm worker` ticks every
# 2s (120 is plenty), a per-minute cron ~180, the optional 5-minute pinger in
# .github/workflows/worker-tick.yml ~900.
# Anything that is not a whole number of seconds in [1, 2592000] (30 days) is
# IGNORED in favour of the default — so a blank value, a typo or a
# seconds/milliseconds mix-up can neither make every deployment permanently
# `down` nor silently disable the check.
# The payload also carries `worker_last_tick_age_seconds` (the raw age, `null`
# when nothing ever ticked): a monitor that must page within minutes should
# threshold that number instead of this boolean.
WORKER_FRESHNESS_SECONDS=

# OPTIONAL (abuse limits). Per-IP capacity of the OTP request bucket.
# Default: 10 per minute. Raise it for a test suite, not for production.
RATE_LIMIT_OTP_CAPACITY=

# OPTIONAL (health). Publish a build/deployment identity in the PUBLIC
# GET /api/health payload. OFF unless this is exactly `true`, because a public
# endpoint should not fingerprint a deployment — the same reason
# `migration_version` is not public (see SECURITY.md / usage-matrix check P1).
# Turn it ON when an internal monitor or a canary needs "which build answered?":
# publishing it there avoids handing that monitor WORKER_TICK_SECRET, which is
# the only other way to read a version out of /api/health.
# When ON, the payload gains exactly one field, `build_id`.
HEALTH_EXPOSE_VERSION=

# OPTIONAL (health). The value published as `build_id` when the flag above is ON:
# your own build identity — a git sha, an image tag, a release name. UNSET → the
# field is `null`, which is honest rather than invented; this repository ships to
# other people's deployments and will not guess one for yours.
APP_BUILD_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL (domain configuration)
#
# The legacy-host redirects exist because the ORIGINAL deployment was first
# served from *.vercel.app hosts and printed QR codes still point there. They are
# therefore off by their nature for anybody else — see SELF_HOSTING.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OPTIONAL (domains). Where legacy hosts are redirected TO. Defaults to
# APP_BASE_URL (your own origin), so a clone never redirects to someone else's
# site.
CANONICAL_ORIGIN=

# OPTIONAL (domains). Comma-separated hosts to redirect FROM. Defaults to the
# two historical Vercel hosts of the upstream deployment. Set it to an empty
# value to redirect from nothing.
LEGACY_REDIRECT_HOSTS=

# OPTIONAL (domains). `off` removes the legacy redirect rules entirely — the
# correct setting unless you actually inherited those hosts.
LEGACY_REDIRECTS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL (development / staging helpers) — DANGEROUS, read the warnings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OPTIONAL (dev convenience). `true` returns the OTP code in the verify response.
# Effective ONLY when APP_ENV=development. Never set it anywhere real.
AUTH_DEV_EXPOSE_OTP=

# OPTIONAL (staging tests). `true` returns the OTP for accounts flagged is_demo
# (the synthetic accounts created by `pnpm db:seed`), so the demo login works with
# no email provider. Off unless exactly `true`; never affects non-demo accounts.
AUTH_EXPOSE_DEMO_OTP=

# OPTIONAL (staging tests, ADR 0009). Master switch for the allowlist below.
# Effective in ANY APP_ENV, including production, where it logs a loud warning.
# NEVER enable this on a real production deployment.
AUTH_EXPOSE_OTP_EMAILS=

# OPTIONAL (staging tests, ADR 0009). Comma-separated addresses whose OTP may be
# returned in the response (the switch above must also be true). Matched against
# the peppered lookup hash; the addresses are never logged. Use synthetic
# addresses only.
AUTH_EXPOSE_OTP_EMAIL_ALLOWLIST=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT READ BY ANY CODE — declared by the provider registry only
#
# These names come from `setup.env` in src/domain/providers.ts and are shown in
# the "how to connect" panel on /me/connections. Setting them changes nothing
# today: their providers are `planned` or `disabled` (no implementation yet, or a
# deliberate policy decision) and a configuration value cannot switch a planned
# provider on. They are listed here only so the connections page and this file do
# not contradict each other.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=      (microsoft-people — planned)
# MICROSOFT_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=  (microsoft-people — planned)
# GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=         (github — planned)
# GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=     (github — planned)
# LINKEDIN_CLIENT_ID=             (linkedin — disabled: policy_restricted)
# LINKEDIN_CLIENT_SECRET=         (linkedin — disabled: policy_restricted)
# LUMA_API_KEY=                   (luma — disabled: awaiting_access)
